  "query_preview": "..\nIssued at 1048 PM CST Mon Nov 2 2026\n\nPeriods of Rain Through Thursday... A large low pressure system will \ncontinue to spin over the region through mid-week keeping on an off \nrain chances in the forecast through Thursday. The heaviest periods \nof rain are expected overnight into Tuesday as southerly winds have \nbrought ample moisture (PWATS above the 99th percentile of \nclimatology) to the region. As winds start to take on more of a \nwesterly component Wednesday gulf moisture will get cut off from the \nregion, however, light on and off showers will likely linger at \nleast through Thursday as forcing associated with the low pressure \nsystem continues over the region. There is a low-end (~5%) for a few \nweak thunderstorms to develop this afternoon, and again Wednesday \nafternoon as weak elevated instability builds over the region.\n\nDespite the prolonged period of rain this week, a complete washout \nis not expected, as showers  will be on and off through Thursday. \nThus far radar estimate show around 0.3-0.7 inches of rain have \nfallen north and west of the Fox Valley with around 0.2 inches haven \nfallen the Fox Valley and along the lakeshore. The probability of \nan addtioanl 1 inch of rain through Thursday us about 20-30% across \nnorthern WI with lower percentages further south. However, depending \non which areas see some convective elements mix in each afternoon \n(i.e. thunder), some localized areas may get closer to two inch \nrainfall. Dry conditions do finally return Friday and Saturday but \nanother short-wave may bring a few showers back on Sunday.\n\nTemperatures...\nThe surge of warm and moist air will keep high temperatures above\nnormal the next couple of days, with 60s forecast for much of the\nregion. The influx of warmer air may also bring some new record\nhigh minimum temperatures for some sites on Tuesday. A slow drop \nis then expected for the latter part of the week with highs getting \nback to the upper 40s to lower 50s and overnight lows falling back \ninto the 20s to 30s.",
